body:
This bug can be easily repeated. I have the following fixture:

https://github.com/hanayashiki/next-action-headers-fixture

The following access to `cookies()` simply doesn't work if `runtime = "edge"`

```js
'use server';

import { cookies } from "next/headers";

export async function act() {
  return cookies().get('token').value;
}

```

Clicking the button the repro throws the error:

```
- error Error: Invariant: Method expects to have requestAsyncStorage, none available
    at act (./src/app/_actions.js:13:65)
```

After reading the compile result, I find this is because the `requestAsyncStorage` accessed by `cookies` is not the one used in `handleAction()`. This only happens in edge runtime, but works well in node. I suspect there's some wrong webpack config that leads to double instantiation of the singleton `requestAsyncStorage`.

Actually, the same error is not limited to `cookie.set()`, it happens with `cookie.get()` too. Plus, it's also not limited to edge runtime, if a server action is using `cookie.set()` or `cookie.get()`, then import in a client component, the error will occur.

Here's the sample code
```tsx
// actions.ts
'use server';
export const someAction = () => {
    const theme = cookies().get('theme');
    // ....
}

// client-component.tsx
'use client';
import {someAction} from './actions';

export const ClientComponent = () => {
    const [isPending, startTransaction] = useTransaction();
    
    const onClick = () => {
        startTransaction(async () => {
            // This will throw `Error: Invariant: Method expects to have requestAsyncStorage, none available`
            await someAction();
        })
    }

     return <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>
}
```

The currency workaround for this is to pass the server action as prop to the client component, instead of importing the server action directly in the client component.
